WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.390 --> 00:00:05.220
After the long-running hit How I Met Your
Mother left the lineup, CBS planned a spinoff,

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:09.300
and even filmed a pilot, but pulled the plug
before the show reached the airwaves.

00:00:09.560 --> 00:00:12.920
Here's a look at the reasons we still haven't
seen How I Met Your Dad.

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:18.180
Though series creators Carter Bays and Craig
Thomas, along with writer Emily Spivey, shot

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.840
a pilot for How I Met Your Dad, CBS kicked
it back for reshoots.

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:26.019
This didn’t sit well with the trio, who
said they wouldn't reshoot the pilot unless

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:28.400
CBS gave a commitment to take it to series.

00:00:28.880 --> 00:00:33.120
CBS pushed back, refusing to give the show
a series order without seeing the reshoots

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:33.620
first.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:38.780
Much to the dismay of How I Met Your Mother
superfans, CBS officially passed on the spinoff

00:00:38.780 --> 00:00:43.360
in May 2014, citing “certain creative elements”
as the reason why.

00:00:46.989 --> 00:00:51.120
When the pilot script for How I Met Your Dad
was released by the website TV Writing in

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:56.620
April 2016, the mystery of why the show was
never picked up became a bit more clear.

00:00:56.850 --> 00:01:01.600
For one, the dialogue was awkward and stilted,
featuring lines like: "Break-ups are like

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:02.600
kidnappings.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.059
The first 48 hours are crucial.

00:01:04.059 --> 00:01:05.630
You gotta blow up the bridge behind you!

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:08.400
And the only way to do it is by doing it … with
a dude."

00:01:08.600 --> 00:01:10.160
And "You're a slob, Sally!

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:11.840
Dishes everywhere, dirty laundry.

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.820
And you always leave cabinet doors open!

00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:15.670
For a while, I thought we had a poltergeist."

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:19.920
Of course, dialogue is almost always worse
when you're reading it off the cold pages

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:24.740
of a script, but these lines felt deeply unnatural,
and fans of How I Met Your Mother had come

00:01:24.740 --> 00:01:26.520
to expect a certain level of quality.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.560
"That’s what passes for comedy these days?

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.200
Whoa, I’m falling back in my chair!

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:32.700
Whoa!

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:33.720
Hee hee hee hee hee!

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:34.340
Whoa!

00:01:34.340 --> 00:01:35.700
I'm really falling back in my chair…"

00:01:35.820 --> 00:01:39.660
The awkward, problematic script might have
been what led to the exit of Broadway actress

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:43.939
Krysta Rodriguez, who was originally set to
play the best friend to Greta Gerwig's lead

00:01:43.940 --> 00:01:44.440
character.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.320
However, Rodriguez reportedly left the show
just days before production was set to begin.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:54.040
Though her departure was never fully explained,
the talented actress, who originated the role

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:58.580
of Wednesday Addams in The Addams Family musical,
could have been a big hit for the series.

00:01:59.140 --> 00:02:03.480
She was replaced by Tiya Sircar, best known
for her roles in The Good Place and Alex,

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:03.980
Inc.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.700
When Greta Gerwig was cast in How I Met Your
Dad’s leading role, many people wondered

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:13.160
how the actress’ signature quirky humor
would fit into the sitcom medium, and fans

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:17.920
were unsure if the indie film star had enough
recognition to carry such an anticipated series.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:22.920
While CBS was reportedly happy with Gerwig’s
portrayal of the lead, they weren't as pleased

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.580
with two of the other members of the five-person
cast.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.500
While declining to name names, anonymous sources
claimed "two of the main characters" were

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:35.080
a sore spot for network executives, who felt
they simply "didn't pop" alongside Gerwig.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:39.580
And though CBS liked Gerwig, the network may
not have been totally satisfied with the lead

00:02:39.580 --> 00:02:40.540
character as a whole.

00:02:41.000 --> 00:02:45.960
Sally, who was described as a "female Peter
Pan who has never grown up and has no idea

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:49.720
of where she's going with her life," might
have been too much of a mess for audiences

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:53.900
to fall in love with, which reportedly was
a point of concern for CBS.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:55.300
"Oh, honey."

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:00.360
Networks take a number of things into consideration
when deciding whether or not to order a series.

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:03.940
And while quality is a big part of it, it’s
not the only thing that matters.

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:09.480
How I Met Your Dad, like How I Met Your Mother,
was a 20th Century Fox property, meaning that

00:03:09.489 --> 00:03:13.940
Fox would make money from an eventual, potentially
lucrative, syndication deal if the series

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.500
took off, not CBS.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:19.800
While this wouldn’t be the only thing stopping
the network from ordering the pilot, it would

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:24.510
definitely put it at a disadvantage against
other comedy pilots that the network was developing

00:03:24.510 --> 00:03:27.700
in-house, like Matthew Perry’s doomed remake
of The Odd Couple.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:32.020
How I Met Your Mother was successful partly
because it was different from every other

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:33.080
sitcom on TV.

00:03:33.660 --> 00:03:37.860
The series had an original and refreshing
format, which made the pilot stand out instantly.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:41.700
However, How I Met Your Dad didn’t benefit
from this originality.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:47.030
The original format was already stale after
nine seasons, and the How I Met Your Dad pilot

00:03:47.030 --> 00:03:48.420
did nothing to revamp it.

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:52.620
Just as How I Met Your Mother started with
Ted talking to his teenage kids about the

00:03:52.629 --> 00:03:57.040
first time he met their Aunt Robin, the How
I Met Your Dad pilot saw Sally recounting

00:03:57.040 --> 00:04:01.970
to her kids the first time she met Frank,
who is assumed to be the titular Dad, before

00:04:01.970 --> 00:04:05.520
finally ending with the big reveal that he's
actually Uncle Frank.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:09.940
While that worked for the original series,
the revamp needed to do something bigger and

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:11.879
bolder in order to set itself apart.

00:04:12.540 --> 00:04:17.100
"I kept this story short and to the point
and you guys still missed it."

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:21.980
Fans lamenting the loss of another chapter
in the How I Met Your Mother saga got an unexpected

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:26.890
surprise in December of 2016, when it was
reported that the spinoff, now officially

00:04:26.890 --> 00:04:29.260
titled How I Met Your Father, was back on.

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.760
Even more encouraging was that the project
had fallen into the hands of Isaac Aptaker

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:38.760
and Elizabeth Berger, two members of the writing
team behind the NBC dramedy This Is Us.

00:04:38.990 --> 00:04:43.960
However, as This Is Us became a bona fide
hit, both Aptaker and Berger found themselves

00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:46.100
unavailable to proceed with How I Met Your
Father.

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:51.700
Fox chairman Dana Walden told Deadline in
August 2017 that the studio would be trying

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:55.660
to develop the spinoff with different writers,
adding that they would be “starting from

00:04:55.660 --> 00:04:59.100
scratch” and not using any elements of the
previous How I Met Your Dad.

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:04.460
Whatever comes of the spinoff, one thing’s
for sure, it has to be legend...Wait for it

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:05.620
"...DARY!

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:07.260
LEGENDARY!"

